# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# Git for Windows checks out with `core.autocrlf=true` by default, and so do GitHub's Windows
# runners: every text file arrives with CRLF. That is harmless for a compiler and fatal here,
# because this application compares literals for a living. `docs/business-rules/13-cross-language-
# contracts.md` lists fourteen strings that are byte-level contracts — prompts, error prefixes,
# keychain key formats, regex sources — and the prompt files under `Ai/Prompts/` are read as data at
# runtime, not compiled. A stray `\r` at the end of a line makes
# "## NIVEL DE REVISIÓN ACTIVO: básico\r" not equal "## NIVEL DE REVISIÓN ACTIVO: básico", which is
# exactly how the first CI run on Windows failed.
#
# So the working tree is LF everywhere, on every platform. The two exceptions below are files
# Windows itself parses, where CRLF is what the tool expects.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows' own script hosts. `cmd.exe` mis-parses a `.bat`/`.cmd` with LF endings — a label or a
# multi-line block can silently run the wrong branch.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touched: normalisation would corrupt them, and a diff of them is noise either way.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.dmg binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.zip binary
*.blockmap binary
